To find the degree measure of the least positive angle \(\theta\) for which

\[
\tan \theta = \frac{\cos 5^\circ \cos 20^\circ + \cos 35^\circ \cos 50^\circ - \sin 5^\circ \sin 20^\circ - \sin 35^\circ \sin 50^\circ}{\sin 5^\circ \cos 20^\circ - \sin 35^\circ \cos 50^\circ + \cos 5^\circ \sin 20^\circ - \cos 35^\circ \sin 50^\circ},
\]

we start by simplifying both the numerator and the denominator using trigonometric identities.

The numerator is:
\[
\cos 5^\circ \cos 20^\circ + \cos 35^\circ \cos 50^\circ - \sin 5^\circ \sin 20^\circ - \sin 35^\circ \sin 50^\circ.
\]
This can be rewritten using the cosine addition formula:
\[
\cos(5^\circ + 20^\circ) + \cos(35^\circ + 50^\circ) = \cos 25^\circ + \cos 85^\circ.
\]

The denominator is:
\[
\sin 5^\circ \cos 20^\circ - \sin 35^\circ \cos 50^\circ + \cos 5^\circ \sin 20^\circ - \cos 35^\circ \sin 50^\circ.
\]
This can be rewritten using the sine addition formula:
\[
\sin(5^\circ + 20^\circ) - \sin(35^\circ + 50^\circ) = \sin 25^\circ - \sin 85^\circ.
\]

Thus, the expression simplifies to:
\[
\tan \theta = \frac{\cos 25^\circ + \cos 85^\circ}{\sin 25^\circ - \sin 85^\circ}.
\]

Using the sum-to-product identities, we simplify the numerator and denominator:
\[
\cos 25^\circ + \cos 85^\circ = 2 \cos 55^\circ \cos 30^\circ,
\]
\[
\sin 25^\circ - \sin 85^\circ = -2 \cos 55^\circ \sin 30^\circ.
\]

Substituting these back into the expression, we get:
\[
\tan \theta = \frac{2 \cos 55^\circ \cos 30^\circ}{-2 \cos 55^\circ \sin 30^\circ} = \frac{\cos 30^\circ}{-\sin 30^\circ} = -\sqrt{3}.
\]

Since \(\tan \theta = -\sqrt{3}\), the angle \(\theta\) must be in the fourth quadrant. The reference angle is 60°, so the angle is \(360^\circ - 60^\circ = 300^\circ\). However, the least positive angle is \(120^\circ\), as it is the smallest positive angle satisfying \(\tan \theta = -\sqrt{3}\).

Thus, the least positive angle \(\theta\) is \(\boxed{120}\) degrees.  2%|▎         | 10/400 [17:34<7:56:51, 73.36s/it]
