Mr. Brennan has 7 boys and 4 girls in his statistics class. He wants to pick 3 boys and 2 girls to give a group presentation tomorrow. The order of selection does not matter, so this is a combination problem.

First, we calculate the number of ways to choose 3 boys out of 7 using the combination formula \( C(n, k) = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \):

\[
C(7, 3) = \frac{7!}{3!(7-3)!} = \frac{7!}{3!4!} = \frac{7 \times 6 \times 5 \times 4!}{3 \times 2 \times 1 \times 4!} = \frac{7 \times 6 \times 5}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = \frac{210}{6} = 35
\]

Next, we calculate the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 4:

\[
C(4, 2) = \frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!} = \frac{4!}{2!2!} = \frac{4 \times 3 \times 2!}{2! \times 2!} = \frac{4 \times 3}{2 \times 1} = \frac{12}{2} = 6
\]

To find the total number of ways to form the group, we multiply the number of ways to choose the boys by the number of ways to choose the girls:

\[
C(7, 3) \times C(4, 2) = 35 \times 6 = 210
\]

Thus, the number of ways Mr. Brennan can pick 3 boys and 2 girls is \(\boxed{210}\).
