To find all \( p \) which satisfy both inequalities \( 0 \ge 54p - 144 \) and \( 0 > 12 - 20p \), we solve each inequality separately and then find the intersection of the solutions.

Starting with the first inequality:
\[ 0 \ge 54p - 144 \]
Add 144 to both sides:
\[ 144 \ge 54p \]
Divide both sides by 54:
\[ \frac{144}{54} \ge p \]
Simplify:
\[ \frac{8}{3} \ge p \]
This can be written as:
\[ p \le \frac{8}{3} \]

Next, solving the second inequality:
\[ 0 > 12 - 20p \]
Subtract 12 from both sides:
\[ -12 > -20p \]
Divide both sides by -20 and reverse the inequality:
\[ \frac{12}{20} < p \]
Simplify:
\[ \frac{3}{5} < p \]
This can be written as:
\[ p > \frac{3}{5} \]

Combining both solutions, we get:
\[ \frac{3}{5} < p \le \frac{8}{3} \]

In interval notation, this is:
\[ \left( \frac{3}{5}, \frac{8}{3} \right] \]

\[
\boxed{\left( \frac{3}{5}, \frac{8}{3} \right]}
\]
