To determine the maximum number of points where at least two of the four distinct circles intersect, we start by noting that each pair of circles can intersect in at most two points. 

First, we calculate the number of pairs of circles. For four circles, the number of pairs is given by the combination formula \( \binom{4}{2} \):

\[
\binom{4}{2} = \frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!} = \frac{4 \times 3}{2 \times 1} = 6
\]

Each pair of circles can intersect at two distinct points. Therefore, the maximum number of intersection points is:

\[
6 \text{ pairs} \times 2 \text{ points per pair} = 12 \text{ points}
\]

We verify that each pair of circles can indeed intersect at two distinct points without any overlap, ensuring that all intersection points are unique. This is achievable by arranging the circles such that each new circle intersects all previous circles at two new points each.

Thus, the maximum number of points where at least two of the circles intersect is \(\boxed{12}\).
