To solve the inequality \(\frac{1}{x - 5} > 0\), we need to determine when the fraction is positive. The numerator is 1, which is positive, so the fraction will be positive if the denominator \(x - 5\) is also positive. 

Solving \(x - 5 > 0\) gives \(x > 5\). We also note that \(x\) cannot be 5 because the denominator would be zero, which is undefined. 

Thus, the solution is all real numbers greater than 5, which in interval notation is \((5, \infty)\).

