To find the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4, we use the concept of the Pisano period. The Pisano period modulo 4 is the length after which the sequence of Fibonacci numbers taken modulo 4 repeats.

First, we compute the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 and identify the repeating cycle:

\[
\begin{align*}
F(1) & = 1 \mod 4 = 1, \\
F(2) & = 1 \mod 4 = 1, \\
F(3) & = (1 + 1) = 2 \mod 4 = 2, \\
F(4) & = (1 + 2) = 3 \mod 4 = 3, \\
F(5) & = (2 + 3) = 5 \mod 4 = 1, \\
F(6) & = (3 + 1) = 4 \mod 4 = 0, \\
F(7) & = (1 + 0) = 1 \mod 4 = 1, \\
F(8) & = (0 + 1) = 1 \mod 4 = 1, \\
F(9) & = (1 + 1) = 2 \mod 4 = 2, \\
F(10) & = (1 + 2) = 3 \mod 4 = 3, \\
F(11) & = (2 + 3) = 5 \mod 4 = 1, \\
F(12) & = (3 + 1) = 4 \mod 4 = 0, \\
F(13) & = (1 + 0) = 1 \mod 4 = 1, \\
F(14) & = (0 + 1) = 1 \mod 4 = 1, \\
F(15) & = (1 + 1) = 2 \mod 4 = 2, \\
F(16) & = (1 + 2) = 3 \mod 4 = 3, \\
F(17) & = (2 + 3) = 5 \mod 4 = 1, \\
F(18) & = (3 + 1) = 4 \mod 4 = 0, \\
F(19) & = (1 + 0) = 1 \mod 4 = 1, \\
F(20) & = (0 + 1) = 1 \mod 4 = 1.
\end{align*}
\]

We observe that the sequence modulo 4 repeats every 6 terms: \(1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0\).

To find the remainder of the 100th term, we compute \(100 \mod 6\):

\[
100 \div 6 = 16 \text{ remainder } 4.
\]

Thus, \(100 \mod 6 = 4\). This means the 100th term modulo 4 is the same as the 4th term modulo 4, which is 3.

Therefore, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \(\boxed{3}\). 59%|█████▉    | 236/400 [4:52:30<3:15:01, 71.35s/it]
