To find the largest integer \( N \) less than 2010 that satisfies the following conditions:
1. \( N \equiv 5 \pmod{7} \)
2. \( N \equiv 10 \pmod{11} \)
3. \( N \equiv 10 \pmod{13} \)

We start by noting that the moduli 7, 11, and 13 are pairwise coprime, so we can apply the Chinese Remainder Theorem. The solution will be of the form \( N = 1001k + c \), where \( c \) is the unique solution modulo 1001.

First, we solve for \( c \) by solving the system:
1. \( c \equiv 5 \pmod{7} \)
2. \( c \equiv 10 \pmod{11} \)
3. \( c \equiv 10 \pmod{13} \)

We find that \( c \equiv 10 \pmod{143} \) because 11 and 13 are coprime, so the solution to \( c \equiv 10 \pmod{11} \) and \( c \equiv 10 \pmod{13} \) is \( c \equiv 10 \pmod{143} \). Thus, \( c = 143m + 10 \).

Next, we solve for \( m \) in the congruence \( 143m + 10 \equiv 5 \pmod{7} \). Since \( 143 \equiv 3 \pmod{7} \) and \( 10 \equiv 3 \pmod{7} \), we get:
\[ 3m + 3 \equiv 5 \pmod{7} \]
\[ 3m \equiv 2 \pmod{7} \]
The multiplicative inverse of 3 modulo 7 is 5, so:
\[ m \equiv 2 \cdot 5 \equiv 10 \equiv 3 \pmod{7} \]
Thus, \( m = 7n + 3 \) for some integer \( n \).

Substituting back, we get:
