To find all the integer roots of the equation \(x^4 + 5x^3 + 9x^2 - x - 14 = 0\), we use the Rational Root Theorem. The possible integer roots are ±1, ±2, ±7, ±14. 

First, we test \(x = 1\):

\[
1^4 + 5 \cdot 1^3 + 9 \cdot 1^2 - 1 - 14 = 1 + 5 + 9 - 1 - 14 = 0
\]

Thus, \(x = 1\) is a root. We factor out \((x - 1)\) using synthetic division:

\[
\begin{array}{r|rrrrr}
1 & 1 & 5 & 9 & -1 & -14 \\
  &   & 1 & 6 & 15 & 14 \\
\hline
  & 1 & 6 & 15 & 14 & 0 \\
\end{array}
\]

The quotient polynomial is \(x^3 + 6x^2 + 15x + 14\). We test \(x = -2\) on this cubic polynomial:

\[
(-2)^3 + 6(-2)^2 + 15(-2) + 14 = -8 + 24 - 30 + 14 = 0
\]

Thus, \(x = -2\) is a root. We factor out \((x + 2)\) using synthetic division:

\[
\begin{array}{r|rrrr}
-2 & 1 & 6 & 15 & 14 \\
   &   & -2 & -8 & -14 \\
\hline
   & 1 & 4 & 7 & 0 \\
\end{array}
\]

The quotient polynomial is \(x^2 + 4x + 7\). The discriminant of this quadratic is \(-12\), which is negative, indicating no real roots. 

Thus, the only integer roots are \(x = 1\) and \(x = -2\).

\[
\boxed{-2}, \boxed{1}
\]
