To determine the primes \( p \) for which the congruence \( 8x \equiv 1 \pmod{p} \) has no solutions, we start by analyzing the conditions under which such a congruence has solutions.

For the congruence \( 8x \equiv 1 \pmod{p} \) to have a solution, 8 must be invertible modulo \( p \). This is true if and only if 8 and \( p \) are coprime. Since \( p \) is a prime, it can only be 2 if 8 is not invertible modulo \( p \).

Checking \( p = 2 \):
- The congruence becomes \( 8x \equiv 1 \pmod{2} \).
- Since 8 is congruent to 0 modulo 2, the equation simplifies to \( 0 \equiv 1 \pmod{2} \), which is false. Therefore, there are no solutions for \( p = 2 \).

For all other primes \( p \), 8 is invertible modulo \( p \) because 8 and \( p \) are coprime. This means the congruence \( 8x \equiv 1 \pmod{p} \) has exactly one solution for each such prime \( p \).

Thus, the only prime for which the congruence has no solutions is \( p = 2 \). Therefore, the sum of all such primes is:

\[
\boxed{2}
\]
