To find the equation of the line on which all vectors \(\mathbf{v}\) lie such that their projection onto the vector \(\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\) is the zero vector, we start by noting that the projection of \(\mathbf{v}\) onto \(\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\) is given by the formula:

\[
\operatorname{proj}_{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \mathbf{v} = \left( \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\left\| \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \right\|^2} \right) \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}
\]

For this projection to be the zero vector, the numerator of the scalar factor must be zero. This means:

\[
\mathbf{v} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = 0
\]

This dot product condition implies that \(\mathbf{v}\) must be orthogonal to the vector \(\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\). Therefore, the set of vectors \(\mathbf{v}\) lies on the line through the origin with a direction vector perpendicular to \(\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\).

The direction vector perpendicular to \(\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\) can be \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}\) or \(\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}\). Both of these vectors have a slope of \(-2\), so the equation of the line is:

\[
y = -2x
\]

Thus, the equation of the line is \(\boxed{y = -2x}\).
